The following is a comprehensive transcript of the second televised leaders' debate of the [[Okaiken general election, 2026|61st General Election]], jointly broadcast by KTK, OR News, and Asahi Okaiken from the Okami National Broadcast Centre on May 10, 2026.

== Background ==
{{main|Okaiken general election, 2026|Satori AI Blackout Crisis|ACTA downgrade}}

The second debate occurred seven days before the May 17 election, in the immediate aftermath of the [[Satori AI Blackout Crisis]] that had paralyzed the nation from May 1–4. The crisis—during which the Satori AI system experienced a catastrophic cascade failure, leaving millions without access to bank accounts, rail services, and emergency communications—fundamentally altered the trajectory of the campaign.

=== The Digital Collapse ===
{{main|Satori AI Blackout Crisis}}
On May 1, 2026, at 09:47 JST, the Satori AI grid experienced a systemic failure that rippled across Okaiken's digital infrastructure. For 72 hours, the nation operated in a state of functional blackout:

* '''Financial services:''' 89 million En-denominated accounts became inaccessible; ATMs returned error codes; the Bank of Okaiken could not process interbank transfers
* '''Transportation:''' The OR network reverted to manual operations at 15% capacity; the Harujima Shinkansen suspended all services
* '''Communications:''' Emergency services reverted to analog radio; mobile networks experienced 60% outages
* '''Government services:''' The Digital Cabinet Office could not access its own records; the Ministry of Health could not process National Universal Health claims

The government declared a state of emergency on May 2, suspending all campaign activities for 72 hours. When the state of emergency was lifted on May 5, the political landscape had fundamentally shifted. The PPO, whose entire platform was built on digital modernization, had collapsed to 15% in the first post-crisis polls. The SCP, under Kousuke Ikazaki's "Fortress Okaiken" doctrine, had surged to second place.

=== The ACTA Downgrade ===
{{main|ACTA credit rating}}
Compounding the crisis, on May 8, the Adonian Credit Transparency Agency (ACTA) announced a historic downgrade of Okaiken's sovereign credit rating from 5 stars to 3 stars—the first such downgrade in the agency's post-war history. The announcement cited:

* "Systemic vulnerability in critical infrastructure"
* "Lack of human redundancy in essential services"
* "Unprecedented concentration of sovereign functions in privately-managed AI systems"
* "Absence of credible disaster recovery protocols"

The downgrade sent shockwaves through the financial markets, with the En falling an additional 8% against the Volisanian mark in after-hours trading. Prime Minister Honda, who had staked his entire political identity on digital transformation, faced the most serious challenge of his career.

