The SDP surge — from 2.8% to 4.9% — was attributed almost entirely to Yoshida's closing statement and the 40% cabinet threshold proposal. Political analysts noted that the surge came primarily from women voters aged 30–60, a demographic that had previously been split between the OSP and LDP.

The combined OSP-SCP vote share of 51.7% in the May 12th polling represented the first time a left-bloc majority had appeared in any survey of the 2026 campaign.

Honda's announcement produced an immediate 3.6-point decline in PPO support, with voters migrating primarily to the LDP.

=== Historical significance ===
The second debate is widely regarded by political historians as the broadcast in which the 2026 election was effectively decided — not by a single moment, but by the accumulation of moments across three hours of broadcast. Honda's resignation announcement. Yoshida's question about women. Nakayama's admission about the 2024 Cabinet meeting. Ikazaki's response to Hasegawa's unscripted question. The public commitments made by every major party to the 40% cabinet threshold and the public inquiry.

Political scientist {{nihongo|Yamamoto Keiko|山本恵子}} of [[Okami University]] wrote in a post-election analysis: "The second debate was not a debate about who should govern. It was a debate about what governing means. And the answer that emerged — imperfectly, across seven candidates and three hours of contested ground — was: governing means being accountable to the people whose lives you affect, including the people who die because of your calculations."

